00:34:51,990 --> 00:34:57,590
the expense but it
is used in a person

490
00:34:57,590 --> 00:35:01,130
suspected of having
mycobacterium tuberculosis.

491
00:35:02,510 --> 00:35:06,150
Serology can be done
for some infections.

492
00:35:06,550 --> 00:35:12,050
Cold agglutination
tests can suggest

493
00:35:12,050 --> 00:35:14,850
mycoplasma although
the diagnosis of

494
00:35:14,850 --> 00:35:18,130
mycoplasma is very
difficult to make. The

495
00:35:24,780 --> 00:35:27,820
treatment of community
-acquired pneumonia depends

496
00:35:27,820 --> 00:35:32,620
on your local resistance
patterns and also the

497
00:35:32,620 --> 00:35:37,040
severity of the illness
for outpatients with no

498
00:35:37,040 --> 00:35:41,860
comorbidities or risk for
MRSA or pseudomonas infection.

499
00:35:42,200 --> 00:35:46,180
The choices are
amoxicillin, doxycycline, or

500
00:35:46,180 --> 00:35:50,580
amacrolide. If the patient
has comorbidities, such as

501
00:35:50,580 --> 00:35:54,860
heart disease, kidney
disease, treatment for cancer,

502
00:35:54,860 --> 00:35:58,440
alcoholism, or chronic
heart or lung disease,

503
00:35:58,860 --> 00:36:01,420
treatment is usually
the combination of

504
00:36:01,420 --> 00:36:04,720
amoxicillin, clavulanate,
or a cephalosporin, and

505
00:36:04,720 --> 00:36:09,000
macrolide, or a respiratory
fluoroquinolone.

506
00:36:09,840 --> 00:36:12,780
Again, you do need
to consider your

507
00:36:12,780 --> 00:36:15,980
local antibiotic
resistance patterns.

508
00:36:16,360 --> 00:36:22,100
for inpatient it also
depends on the severity and

509
00:36:22,100 --> 00:36:24,620
especially whether the
patient needs to go to

510
00:36:24,620 --> 00:36:27,640
the intensive care unit
but for the most part

511
00:36:27,640 --> 00:36:31,980
treatment is a beta-lactam
plus a macrolide or respiratory

512
00:36:31,980 --> 00:36:35,780
fluoroquinolone with
the addition of MRSA

513
00:36:35,780 --> 00:36:39,260
or pseudomonas coverage
if the patient is at risk

514
00:36:47,510 --> 00:36:50,150
prevention of immune
acquired pneumonia

515
00:36:50,150 --> 00:36:53,830
includes managing
comorbidities for example

516
00:36:53,830 --> 00:36:58,530
controlling blood sugars
and having the hemoglobin

517
00:36:58,530 --> 00:37:02,350
a1c at goal in patients
who have diabetes

518
00:37:03,190 --> 00:37:05,970
restoring the immune
system in patients

519
00:37:05,970 --> 00:37:10,370
with hiv to get people
to stop smoking and

520
00:37:11,850 --> 00:37:15,050
control COPD as much as

521
00:37:15,050 --> 00:37:18,690
possible and to control asthma.

522
00:37:19,830 --> 00:37:23,830
Patients with cognitive
decline or neuromuscular

523
00:37:23,830 --> 00:37:27,570
disorders or other risk
factors for aspiration

524
00:37:28,590 --> 00:37:33,770
should have interventions
to help prevent

525
00:37:33,770 --> 00:37:38,590
aspiration and of
course, most importantly,

526
00:37:38,590 --> 00:37:42,150
vaccination both with
pneumococcal vaccines and

527
00:37:42,150 --> 00:37:45,950
with a yearly influenza
vaccine. Remember,

528
00:37:46,170 --> 00:37:51,230
the pneumococcal vaccines are
indicated in some patients

529
00:37:51,230 --> 00:37:57,030
with risk, including those
without spleens, sickle

530
00:37:57,030 --> 00:38:01,110
cell disease, chronic lung,
heart, kidney disease,

531
00:38:01,390 --> 00:38:05,390
and other forms of immune
suppression, such as HIV.

532
00:38:06,570 --> 00:38:12,070
and if that vaccine is
given before 65 years

533
00:38:12,070 --> 00:38:18,030
of age and it is again
repeated five years

534
00:38:18,030 --> 00:38:24,940
later or if the patient
is 65 at that time or

535
00:38:24,940 --> 00:38:29,250
when the patient does
reach 65 years of age.

536
00:38:31,080 --> 00:38:34,210
The influenza vaccine, of
course, should be given

537
00:38:34,210 --> 00:38:39,210
every year to everyone
over six months of age.

538
00:38:46,680 --> 00:38:49,480
Let's talk a little bit
about the difference between

539
00:38:49,480 --> 00:38:52,780
typical and atypical
community-acquired pneumonia.

540
00:38:53,540 --> 00:38:57,700
The typical organisms that
cause community-acquired

541
00:38:57,700 --> 00:39:00,820
pneumonia include Streptococcus
pneumoniae, Haemophilus

542
00:39:00,820 --> 00:39:03,440
influenzae, Klebsiella
pneumoniae, Staphylococcus

543
00:39:03,440 --> 00:39:06,880
aureus, Marexella catarralis,
and Pseudomonas aeruginosa.

544
00:39:07,800 --> 00:39:11,400
As opposed to the atypical
organisms, these can all

545
00:39:11,400 --> 00:39:16,120
be seen on gram stain and
cultured using usual media.

546
00:39:16,560 --> 00:39:19,880
As you can see the
list of organisms that

547
00:39:19,880 --> 00:39:25,780
cause atypical pneumonia,
the opposite is true.

548
00:39:26,360 --> 00:39:30,380
These organisms normally
can't be seen on gram stain

549
00:39:30,380 --> 00:39:36,640
or use fastidious types
of culture media in order

550
00:39:36,640 --> 00:39:40,600
to be grown or can't be
grown at all in the lab.

551
00:39:41,340 --> 00:39:45,500
The most common causes of
typical pneumonia include

552
00:39:45,500 --> 00:39:48,200
streptococcus pneumoniae
and haemophilus influenzae.

553
00:39:48,600 --> 00:39:51,300
And mycoplasma
pneumoniae is by far

554
00:39:51,300 --> 00:39:54,500
the most common cause
of typical pneumonia,

555
00:39:54,500 --> 00:39:56,200
or I'm sorry,
atypical pneumonia.

556
00:39:57,420 --> 00:40:02,100
And in general, the
presentation of the two is

557
00:40:02,100 --> 00:40:06,020
different, although this is
not a hard and fast rule.

558
00:40:06,460 --> 00:40:10,860
Typical community cord
pneumonia is acute

559
00:40:10,860 --> 00:40:16,540
in onset and usually
has a productive cough,

560
00:40:17,100 --> 00:40:25,280
dyspnea, and pleuritic
chest pain. you will find on

561
00:40:25,280 --> 00:40:29,840
physical examination crackles
and changes in breath

562
00:40:29,840 --> 00:40:34,520
sounds and percussion and
there is usually a clear

563
00:40:34,520 --> 00:40:39,220
consolidation on chest x
-ray the atypical organisms

564
00:40:40,080 --> 00:40:43,320
sometimes called walking
pneumonia although this

565
00:40:43,320 --> 00:40:45,620
is certainly not true for
all of these organisms

566
00:40:45,620 --> 00:40:53,140
is of slow onset that the
symptoms are usually not

567
00:40:53,140 --> 00:40:56,260
as severe, the cough is
usually not productive,

568
00:40:57,020 --> 00:40:59,640
and very often physical

569
00:40:59,640 --> 00:41:02,020
examination
findings are absent.

570
00:41:02,410 --> 00:41:05,420
For some of these,
the chest x-ray

571
00:41:05,420 --> 00:41:07,260
does look worse
than the patient,

572
00:41:07,800 --> 00:41:11,960
but it needs to be
stressed that, in

573
00:41:12,420 --> 00:41:14,560
truth, you cannot
tell the difference

574
00:41:14,560 --> 00:41:17,640
between these organisms
by chest x-ray.

575
00:41:19,160 --> 00:41:23,960
And certainly there are
some organisms in the

576
00:41:23,960 --> 00:41:28,020
atypical group that can
cause very severe disease

577
00:41:28,020 --> 00:41:34,940
such as Hantavirus and
RSV and Legionella.

578
00:41:35,260 --> 00:41:40,140
Many of the organisms in the
atypical group only occur

579
00:41:40,140 --> 00:41:44,900
in people who are immune
suppressed or at risk. In

580
00:41:51,990 --> 00:41:56,790
summary, we've talked
about the impact of upper

581
00:41:56,790 --> 00:42:00,170
respiratory tract
infections and the role of

582
